{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TfwQVw7PreZbT14a2MVwxF","type":"tool_result","content":"func TestEntropy(t *testing.T) {\n\tif e := entropy([]byte(\"aaaa\")); e != 0 {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"entropy = %v\", e)\n\t}\n\tif e := entropy([]byte(randStr(50, 64))); e < 4 {\n\t\tt.Errorf(\"entropy = %v\", e)\n\t}\n\t_ = fmt.Sprint\n}\n\nfunc TestScannerGenericAssignmentFalsePositives(t *testing.T) {\n\tfor _, in := range []string{\n\t\t\"Tokens: u.Tokens\",\n\t\t\"Token: w.Token\",\n\t\t\"Token: w.WorkerToken.Value\",\n\t\t\"TotalTokens: usage.TotalTokens,\",\n\t\t`\"cache_read_input_tokens\": 1106888`,\n\t\t`\"tokens\": 11068881106888123`,\n\t} {\n\t\tif f := scanString(t, in); len(f) != 0 {\n\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"%q: unexpected findings %v\", in, f)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc TestScannerGenericAssignmentTruePositives(t *testing.T) {\n\tfor _, in := range []string{\n\t\t\"api_\" + \"key = \" + randStr(13, 32),\n\t\t\"token: \\\"\" + randStr(14, 32) + \"\\\"\",\n\t\t\"password = \\\"\" + randStr(15, 24) + \".\" + randStr(16, 24) + \"\\\"\",\n\t\t\"secret=\" + randStr(17, 40) + \".\" + randStr(18, 40),\n\t} {\n\t\tfound := false\n\t\tfor _, f := range scanString(t, in) {\n\t\t\tif f.Rule == \"generic-assignment\" {\n\t\t\t\tfound = true\n\t\t\t}\n\t\t}\n\t\tif !found {\n\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"%q: not flagged\", in)\n\t\t}\n\t}\n}\n\nfunc scanString(t *testing.T, in string) []Finding {\n\tt.Helper()\n\tf, err := Builtin().Scan(context.Background(), strings.NewReader(in+\"\\n\"))\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t}\n\treturn f\n}\n\nfunc TestScannerAWSExample(t *testing.T) {\n\tsuffix := \"EX\" + \"AMPLE\"\ninternal/redact/scan.go:101:\t\t\t\tif !quoted && (codeRef(v) || numeric(v)) {\ninternal/redact/scan.go:104:\t\t\t\tif placeholder(v) || entropy(v) < entropyThreshold {\ndocs/TODO.md:21:- #254 Log secret scan: generic-assignment flags token counts and Token fields\ndocs/site/security.md:10:- **Log redaction.** Before a log is committed, close-out removes known secret values and replaces the host's own paths (the worktree, the home directory, the user name) with fixed placeholders, so logs do not reveal the machine (#156). A log that still fails the secret scan is held back. Logs are capped at 1 MiB.\ndocs/proposal.md:133:**Fetched inputs (#300).** A worker that fetches bytes from the network records each one under `fetched:` in its role file. Close-out parses the list strictly, like `findings`: an entry with a non-https URL (or one with userinfo, whitespace, backticks or angle brackets), a bad date or status, a `sha256` that is not 64 lowercase hex characters, a `verified_by` that is neither `none` nor an https URL, an unclean or missing `path`, or an unknown key fails the parse, as do more than 50 entries and a `fetched` key on a non-worker role file. The worker PR body then lists the entries under **Fetched inputs** (URL, landing path, sha256, verified_by; `none` shows as unverified) from the parsed fields only, never from free text; the list is dropped, like the summary, when redaction or the secret scan touches it. The human-readable record beside each fixture set is a `CAPTURE.md` with the same fields as a table plus the regen command (the #47 precedent); the worker writes it as part of the change, and ard does not parse it.\ndocs/proposal.md:180:**All agent output is committed too.** Each run's full output lands in `.ard/<issue#>/logs/<stage>-<role>-<ts>.log`. Close-out redacts known secret values from the whole stream and, in the same pass, replaces the controller host's own paths with fixed placeholders: the job worktree becomes `/work`, the job home `/ard-home`, the executor root `~/.ard`, the operator's home `~` and the controller's temp dir `$TMPDIR` (skipped when it is a shared `/tmp`). Each path is also replaced in its symlink-resolved form, longest first, and only where it ends at a path boundary; bare usernames are not touched. Close-out then secret-scans the whole redacted log (the scan sees the replaced text), then caps it at 1 MiB: the first 32 KiB plus the last ~992 KiB, cut on line boundaries and joined by one truncation marker line. The full redacted log stays host-local for 7 days. Git LFS is not used (decided, #4); revisit only if `.git` growth demands it. Once the redacted copy is committed, the raw log is deleted. Triage output goes to `main` through the committer; worker and reviewer output goes on the branch and ships in the PR. **Scanner run logs stay host-local in phase 0** (`~/.ard/logs/`, 7 days) and are not committed: one run can touch many issues, and close-out redaction and the secret scan (#20, #21) land after Gate 1. What a scanner run commits is `.ard/<issue#>/issue.md` for each issue it files or joins, nothing else. Committing scanner logs, redacted, to each touched issue is a follow-up. Never committed: scanner state databases, and any log the secret scan flags. **Optional gitleaks pass (#89).** With `redact.gitleaks.enabled: true` (default false; `redact.gitleaks.path` names the pinned binary, default `gitleaks` on PATH, and is read at each close-out) the scan runs a second pass after the builtin one: `gitleaks detect --no-git --report-format json` over the redacted bytes, in a private temp dir with a scrubbed environment and a 2-minute timeout. Findings keep only the rule, line and column, never the matched text. The pass fails closed: a missing binary, an error exit, a timeout or an unreadable report means nothing is committed. A hit is treated like an internal hit: the log stays host-local for 7 days and the job gets `ard:needs-human`. `redact.gitleaks.block_autoclose_when_missing` (default false) is read by the scanner guard, which runs the same pass over findings: when set and gitleaks cannot run, scanner autoclose stops for that run too.\ndocs/proposal.md:256:**The trufflehog scanner** (`scanners/trufflehog`, a `sh` script plus one `jq` program) runs `trufflehog filesystem . --json --no-update --no-color --no-verification --no-ignore-tag --fail-on-scan-errors --concurrency=2` on the current commit. It never verifies a credential against a provider. Only the detector name, file, line and a hash of the secret leave the scan: `Raw`, `RawV2`, `Redacted`, `SecretParts`, `ExtraData` and `StructuredData` stay in pipes inside `scan`. The fingerprint is `trufflehog:` plus the first 16 hex digits of SHA-256 over `ard-trufflehog-v1`, a NUL byte and the base64 of the secret (an unsalted hash, so one value gives one issue wherever it appears). The scan fails closed: a non-zero trufflehog exit, a missing `finished scanning` summary, zero chunks on a non-empty tree, or a result count that differs from the summary produces no findings, so nothing is autoclosed on a partial run. `exclude-paths` (regexes; `.git` is always excluded) and `allow` (one fingerprint per line) are human-owned; a bad `allow` entry fails the run. Findings use `route: needs-human`, severity high, and carry a runbook that says to rotate the credential first. `commit` is the scanned SHA, recorded in `.ard/<n>/issue.md`; history is scanned by `scanners/trufflehog-history`. The forbidden flags (`--since-commit`, `--max-depth`, `--detector-timeout`, `--archive-timeout`, `--filter-unverified`, `--filter-entropy`, `--results`) are never passed, because a partial or load-dependent result would autoclose live findings. Caveats: (1) a secret gone from HEAD is not revoked; autoclose closes the issue as completed, so the issue body tells the human to rotate first. (2) The fingerprint hash is unsalted, an offline oracle for a low-entropy value that is already in the repo at HEAD; accepted. (3) Issues on a public repo show `file:line`. (4) At `max_open` the scanner is skipped, so new leaks go unseen until a human resolves one; scanner failure streaks are posted on the status issue (#221). (5) Any future change that commits scanner logs must exclude this scanner. (6) On a public repo with GitHub secret scanning off, this scanner is the only detection channel, and it cannot stop a push. TruffleHog is AGPL-3.0: it is installed by the role's Dockerfile (or a host install) and is not in the published `ard` image or the root `Dockerfile`. Host and Docker integration tests (`go test -tags integration -run Trufflehog ./internal/roles/`, with `ARD_TRUFFLEHOG_BIN` at the Dockerfile's pinned version) prove the role against a real binary and image.\ndocs/proposal.md:361:5. **Guard the diff** after each agent or fix step, before anything leaves the host. The worktree belongs to the agent, so the guard never trusts its `.git`, config, index or attributes: git runs against the controller's own clone with a private index filled from the base commit (so `skip-worktree` and `assume-unchanged` hide nothing, and no planted filter, textconv, fsmonitor or hook runs), with its objects in a private temp directory (the clone is only read, so an agent cannot grow it); a worktree with a new or resized file over 1 MiB, or more than 256 of them, is held before anything is hashed. The scanned bytes are copied to a controller-private directory and the code lane commits the copies, so nothing can change a file between the scan and the commit. Close-out's changed-file listing uses the same hardened path. Paths under `.ard/<issue#>/` and `.ard-input/` belong to close-out and the loop and are left out. Everything else is checked, and any rejection holds the job for a human (`ard:needs-human`, nothing pushed): the path rule in `committer.CheckCodePath` (all of `.github/`, the rest of `.ard/`, `.git` segments, `.gitmodules`, any `.gitattributes` (`linguist-generated` or `-diff` would collapse files in the GitHub PR diff, so a worker change to one always goes to a human), secret-shaped names), any symlink, nested repository or other non-regular file, a file over 1 MiB, a binary file, and more than 256 changed files. The credential scan runs twice: the content of every written file is checked for the values of known secrets (the hold names 